Sympathetic Nervous System Overdrive / Dysautonomia
Integrative & Functional medicine
Inability to physically relax (chronic stress), Unprovoked palpitations/tachycardia at rest, Profound orthostatic intolerance, Diminished heart rate variability (HRV)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-045
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unprovoked resting tachycardia, profound dizziness upon standing, and intense chest tightness requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute cardiac arrhythmia or acute coronary syndrome.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden, extreme heart racing at rest (palpitations), near-syncope when attempting to walk, and an inability to catch their breath. Urgent on-site physical and cardiovascular assessment was strictly required to exclude supraventricular tachycardia (SVT), atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response, or acute myocardial ischemia, preventing severe hemodynamic compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heart is hammering out of my chest while I'm just sitting here, and if I try to stand up, the room goes completely black."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a rapid escalation of severe, distinctly unprovoked palpitations, sudden unexplained severe racing heart, and a distinct severe intense feeling of a 'pounding' chest entirely at rest, beginning 2 hours ago. The patient also complains of profound, distinct, and extreme specific orthostatic intolerance directly triggered strictly by assuming an upright posture, leading to severe dizziness, intense lightheadedness, and frequent distinct presyncope upon minimal exertion today. The patient reports chronic, distinctly unresolving high levels of severe physical and psychological stress, intense generalized severe anxiety, and a distinct profound absolute inability to physically relax. Symptoms include vague, diffuse, and mild chest tightness, frequent distinct intense severe rapid shallow breathing, and insomnia characterized by a 'tired but wired' sensation. The patient also notes recent sudden distinct severe intense specific chemical sensitivities.
Functional Status: Patient is lying flat on the floor, legs elevated on a chair, highly anxious, and unable to safely stand or walk to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; extremely high fall risk due to severe orthostatic presyncope.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 (Supine), 105/70 (Standing - delayed) | HR: 95 (Supine), 135 (Standing) | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is pale, visibly anxious, diaphoretic, and hyperventilating slightly while lying on the floor.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular, sinus tachycardia at rest (95 bpm). Crucially, upon having the patient carefully stand, the heart rate acutely spikes to 135 bpm within 2 minutes, accompanied by a moderate drop in blood pressure and severe symptomatic dizziness (presyncope), confirming profound orthostatic intolerance (consistent with Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome - POTS or severe dysautonomia). Heart sounds are normal; no murmurs or irregular rhythms (ruling out AFib). Respiratory exam shows clear air entry bilaterally; the chest tightness is muscular/anxiety-driven. Neurological exam is intact. The presentation perfectly aligns with the tetrad of inability to physically relax, unprovoked resting tachycardia, and profound orthostatic intolerance. Confirmation requires formal tilt-table testing and autonomic monitoring (diminished HRV).
Environmental Observations: Room is warm. Patient has consumed two large coffees today, exacerbating the sympathetic drive.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular cardiac medications (beta-blockers) or anxiolytics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sympathetic Nervous System Overdrive / Dysautonomia (Acute POTS/Autonomic Flare) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) / Atrial Fibrillation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Auscultated rhythm is distinctly regular sinus tachycardia, and the heart rate fluctuates directly with postural changes (orthostatic) rather than being a fixed, sudden-onset electrical arrhythmia >150 bpm.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is vague/mild chest tightness rather than crushing central pressure, clear correlation with posture and hyperventilation, and normal oxygenation.

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Perfect room air oxygen saturation, clear lung fields, and absence of unilateral leg swelling or DVT risk factors.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe resting tachycardia, profound orthostatic presyncope, and chest tightness. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent, time-critical need to evaluate for a life-threatening acute cardiac arrhythmia (SVT, AFib) or acute myocardial ischemia. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to auscultate the heart rhythm, perform dynamic postural vital signs (lying/standing), and assess hemodynamic stability. The physical confirmation of a regular sinus rhythm and the specific, profound orthostatic spike in heart rate confirmed the absence of a primary electrical arrhythmia or immediate cardiogenic shock. However, the severity of the dysautonomia, the functional inability to stand without fainting, and the unbearable psychological panic dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of syncopal head trauma, severe hyperventilation, and immense physical suffering. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to remain supine. Administered oral hydration with electrolytes on-site. Guided the patient through physiological vagal maneuvers (bearing down/Valsalva) to acutely lower the heart rate. Recommend urgent GP follow-up for a formal cardiology referral, 24-hour Holter monitor, and potential initiation of beta-blockers or Ivabradine.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically manage the profound orthostatic intolerance and sympathetic overdrive, initiate strict fluid and postural protocols. Action: Consume 500ml of water containing 1/2 teaspoon of salt immediately before attempting to stand up in the morning or after prolonged sitting. Consequence: Rapid, high-sodium fluid intake dramatically expands intravascular blood volume, directly preventing blood from pooling in the legs upon standing, explicitly rewarding the patient by stopping the massive compensatory heart rate spike and dizziness. Action: Wear medical-grade (20-30 mmHg) waist-high compression tights daily. Consequence: External mechanical compression of the lower extremities physically forces venous return back to the heart, directly counteracting the autonomic failure and rewarding the patient with the ability to stand safely.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provide clear instructions on crawling or scooting to the bathroom tonight if dizziness is severe.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for cardiology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing a sudden, fixed heart rate over 150 bpm that does not change with resting, sudden crushing central chest pain, complete loss of consciousness (fainting), or a sudden inability to catch your breath while lying completely flat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute heart racing and dizziness are caused by a glitch in your autonomic nervous system, not a heart attack. Managing this requires strict adherence to hydration and physical counter-maneuvers.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care (Call 000) if you actually pass out completely, if the chest tightness turns into a crushing weight, or if your heart rate suddenly jumps incredibly high and stays there even when lying flat.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body's automatic blood pressure control system is malfunctioning. When you stand, your blood vessels aren't tightening properly, so blood pools in your legs. Your heart then has to beat incredibly fast to pump enough oxygen to your brain, causing the dizziness and panic.
Actionable Steps: Drink salty fluids immediately. Do not attempt to stand up quickly tonight; sit on the edge of the bed for 2 minutes before standing. You must see your GP this week to organize a formal heart monitor and discuss specific medications to regulate this system.

